Ok, so `mysql2`'s `query` method starts by calling `rb_mysql_client_set_active_thread(self);`

```c
void rb_mysql_client_set_active_thread(VALUE self) {
  VALUE thread_current = rb_thread_current();
  GET_CLIENT(self);

  // see if this connection is still waiting on a result from a previous query
  if (NIL_P(wrapper->active_thread)) {
    // mark this connection active
    wrapper->active_thread = thread_current;
  } else if (wrapper->active_thread == thread_current) {
    rb_raise(cMysql2Error, "This connection is still waiting for a result, try again once you have the result");
  } else {
    VALUE inspect = rb_inspect(wrapper->active_thread);
    const char *thr = StringValueCStr(inspect);

    rb_raise(cMysql2Error, "This connection is in use by: %s", thr);
  }
}
```

Since we're in C, we're protected by the GVL, so this is thread safe.

What is less clear is whether this is released atomically.

On the happy path, the "lock" is released in a function that is called without the GVL:

```c
static void *nogvl_do_result(void *ptr, char use_result) {
  mysql_client_wrapper *wrapper = ptr;
  MYSQL_RES *result;

  if (use_result) {
    result = mysql_use_result(wrapper->client);
  } else {
    result = mysql_store_result(wrapper->client);
  }

  /* once our result is stored off, this connection is
     ready for another command to be issued */
  wrapper->active_thread = Qnil;

  return result;
}
```

I won't dig much more than that, but I think:

  - That `mysql2` lock is a bit racy and could be improved.
  - It lock on a per thread basis, which means it probably blows up with the fiber scheduler (does the fiber scheduler even work with mysql2?)

So yeah, I think your app does use the connection concurrently. It would be worth monkey patching mysql2 to dump all threads backtraces when this happens.
